  "Question": "The arithmetic mean between \\(\\dfrac{x+a}{x}\\) and \\(\\dfrac{x-a}{x},\\) when \\(x\\ne0,\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(2,\\) if \\(a\\ne0\\)",
    "B": "\\(1\\)",
    "C": "\\(1,\\) only if \\(a=0\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac ax\\)",
    "E": "\\(x\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "The arithmetic mean of two numbers is their sum divided by \\(2\\). Adding first is useful here because the \\(+a\\) and \\(-a\\) terms cancel:\n\\[\n\\frac12\\left(\\frac{x+a}{x}+\\frac{x-a}{x}\\right)\n=\\frac{(x+a)+(x-a)}{2x}\n=\\frac{2x}{2x}=1.\n\\]\nThe cancellation is valid because \\(x\\ne0\\). This holds for every \\(a\\), including \\(a=0\\), so the answer is \\(1\\), choice B.",
